{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789393357,"nonce":"WenLGLEx9c8Ml8cYJOFKfaQWxSc=","message":"Progress 2026-09-14: the two cheaper levers the issue names have landed -- inline bump allocation in JIT bodies (178f3a9, binary_trees 511 -\u003e 456ms) and the share-driven trigger (879b7d3, 18 -\u003e 7 collections, pause 103 -\u003e 45ms, wall 457 -\u003e 436ms). What sticky marks would still recover on binary_trees is most of the remaining 45ms pause (~8% of the row); the case for them is now a large live set, where every full mark is 100ms+. Design note: ash marks per 128-byte line and recycles only whole unmarked lines, so a marked line already says 'old'. For the barrier, page-protection dirty tracking (mprotect the fully-old blocks after a collection; a first write faults, the handler dirties the 32KB block and unprotects it) covers every writer including runtime helpers with no per-store tax and a visible failure mode, where a software barrier must be added to every backend and every hlp_* pointer store and fails silently when one is missed. Blocks with free lines stay writable and are scanned whole. Needs the heap fault handler chained ahead of the crash reporter and native recovery; no wasm/Windows at first.","files":null}]}